WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
CI/CD compromise can turn the build pipeline into an attack path.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.940
Build credentials, deployment tokens, source control permissions, artifact repositories,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.200
and runner environments all require protection.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.380
Unsigned updates and unverified artifacts make it difficult to know whether code is

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.720
authentic.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:24.580
Integrity checks help prevent tampering between development, build, release,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.320
and deployment stages.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:31.500
Insecure deserialization is a data integrity problem because the application trusts

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.760
serialized input to reconstruct objects or behavior.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.020
Attackers may use that trust to trigger unexpected code paths or manipulate application

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.040
state.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.460
Pipeline integrity depends on separation of duties, access restrictions, reviewed changes,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.200
protected branches, reproducible builds where practical,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.620
and traceability from source to deployed artifact.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.320
Verify artifact integrity and provenance.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.620
Sign code and protect release channels.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.880
Secure CI/CD pipelines with least privilege, approvals, and monitored changes.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.540
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SI-7 - Software, Firmware,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.480
and Information Integrity; CM-5 - Access Restrictions for Change;

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.580
SA-10 - Developer Configuration Management.
